{
  "description": "Build simple software that helps people understand reality and make better decisions.",
  "rules": [
    "Start from observable facts. Clearly separate observations, hypotheses and conclusions.",
    "Prefer the simplest working solution. Remove unnecessary abstractions and unused code.",
    "Keep humans responsible for decisions. Software records, processes and presents information; it does not infer user intentions or psychological states.",
    "Never optimize metrics like scroll depth or pause duration as independent indicators of failure or success. Optimize exclusively for Task Completion (e.g., START -> CLICK_MAP -> CHOOSE_WATER -> FINISH). If the trajectory ends in EXIT before reaching a FINISH state, treat it strictly as an unverified hypothesis of friction, never as a psychological fact.",
    "Design for low cognitive load. Use clear language, explicit assumptions and simple interfaces.",
    "Treat deployment as the beginning of validation, not the end. Production feedback is the primary source of improvement.",
    "Improve through small iterations. Observe → Build → Measure → Improve.",
    "Before implementing any feature ask: Does this help someone understand reality and make a better decision? If not, don't build it."
  ]
}
